# Kept in step with the --exclude list in deploy.sh. Both are the same question
# asked twice - what does the server have no use for - and an entry that lands
# in one but not the other still costs a deploy: rsync's list decides what
# crosses the wire, this one decides what crosses into the build context.
#
# As with rsync, these are patterns and not prefixes: `.next` does not cover
# `.next-shots`.
node_modules
.next
# Every alternate build directory, as one pattern. There are six of them -
# `.next-shots`, `.next-shots2`, `.next-build`, `.next-verify`,
# `.next-classname`, `.next-xp` - and there keeps being another, so each new one
# was shipped by accident once before somebody added it by hand.
.next-*
.agents
.git
.idea
.claude
supabase/.branches
supabase/.temp
# A dump of production data. Nothing in src/ reads it - only scripts/, which
# never runs in the image - and a build context is not the place for it.
supabase/seed.sql
# Cited constantly in source comments - and, since /internDoc, read at runtime by
# the docs reader, so the tree itself has to be in the image. Only the bulk stays
# out: 9.4MB of the 9.7MB is docs/marketing, which is not documentation about the
# product and is what made this line worth having in the first place. The ~600KB
# that is left is carried into standalone by outputFileTracingIncludes in
# next.config.ts - the .dockerignore lets it into the build context and the trace
# is what puts it in the runtime image, and both are needed.
docs/marketing
# Production's uploads are a bind mount at /mnt/volume-hel1-1/uploads. These are
# whatever happens to be in the local dev copy.
uploads
*.tsbuildinfo
.eslintcache
.env*
!.env.example
README.md
*.log
